PSEUDOTERMINAL (PTY) — PICONIX

PicoNIX provides one general-purpose PTY pair without network sockets:
  /dev/ptmx  master (open with O_RDWR, one exclusive pair)
  /dev/pts/0  slave (open after /dev/ptmx)

To run an interactive child, save and redirect FDs 0, 1 and 2 to
/dev/pts/0, call spawn_foreground(), then restore the parent's FDs.
The child uses PTY controlling TTY 3; the parent retains its own TTY.

write(master) -> input to slave (canonical/echo processing)
read(master)  <- output from slave (including raw ANSI sequences)
The slave supports TTY ioctl(), fcntl(O_NONBLOCK), and select().
Master reads can block until output arrives. With a full input queue,
master write may return OS_EAGAIN and should be retried.
Slave output uses a 1024-byte ring and can block until the master reads.
Closing the last master makes slave reads reach EOF and writes fail.
Both ends must close before a new session can be allocated.

PTY is a generic kernel feature. MCP terminal interaction is not yet
implemented; the kernel does not emulate an ANSI terminal screen.

The standard pty_test command runs tty on the PTY by default.
Use pty_test fd or pty_test vi /tmp/test.txt to forward keyboard
input and ANSI output between the console and a PTY child.
Ctrl+C reaches the PTY foreground task (forced termination, not SIGINT).
pty_test restores its console settings after the child exits.
